costs.py's scenario B: a log kept a year has ~3,469 partitions, so batches average 1.15
events, and ~119 shards write each page_daily row within the prune window. costs.py's row,
0.3 KB plus the marks, is 3.75 KB; this one is smaller, but bills the same 4 write units.
The stream report's figures are costs.py's for scenario B: 9,435 write and 4,300 read units
per second, and $27,272 a month per region.
-- dynaflux.yaml --
store: analytics
regions: [us-east-1, us-west-2, ap-northeast-1]
runtime:
  timeout_seconds: 60
  prune_window_seconds: 120
-- dynaflux/page_views.sql --
CREATE STREAM page_views (user_id String, page String, duration_ms UInt32)
PRIMARY KEY (user_id)
LOG RETENTION 365 DAY;
-- dynaflux/views.sql --
CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW page_daily
ORDER BY (page, day)
PRIMARY KEY (page)
AS SELECT page, toStartOfDay(event_time) AS day, count() AS views, sum(duration_ms) AS total_ms
FROM page_views
GROUP BY page, day;

CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW user_latest
ORDER BY (user_id)
AS SELECT user_id, argMax(page, event_time) AS last_page
FROM page_views
GROUP BY user_id;

CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW user_daily
ORDER BY (user_id, day)
AS SELECT user_id, toStartOfDay(event_time) AS day, count() AS views, uniqExact(page) AS pages
FROM page_views
GROUP BY user_id, day;
-- explain page_views --
stream page_views
  assumptions  --rate 1000  --event-kb 1  --rows page_daily=1000  --uniq-new user_daily=0.3
               (traffic isn't in the SQL or dynaflux.yaml; set it with these flags)
  prices       us-east-1 list prices, ±30%: for comparing designs, not a quote; prices in ap-northeast-1 are higher

layout, in each of 3 regions (us-east-1, us-west-2, ap-northeast-1)
  table        analytics-stream-page_views: Global Table, on-demand
  key          pk user_id; sk event_time, event_id
  log          LOG RETENTION 365 DAY: ~34,690 GB in ~3,469 partitions, each a change-stream shard
  projector    batching window 1 s, batches of at most 100 events: ~869 invocations/s, 1.15 events each on average
  marks        a view row keeps a shard's high-water mark for 120 s after the shard's progress passes it (runtime.prune_window_seconds)
  archive      Firehose to Iceberg in us-east-1, us-west-2, ap-northeast-1

per event, per region                                     write units  read units
  event put, local or replicated                                 1.00
  TTL delete, replicated from the region that expires it         0.67
  page_daily, shared rows, 1,000 active                          3.30        1.10
  user_latest, a row per entity                                  1.10        1.10
  user_daily, a row per entity                                   1.10        1.10
    uniqExact markers                                            0.30        1.00
  progress item, one per batch                                   0.87
  total                                                          8.34        4.30
  per second, all events                                        8,335       4,300

monthly                        per region  3 regions
  DynamoDB requests               $15,103    $45,309
    stream and meta tables         $4,164    $12,493
    page_daily                     $5,781    $17,343
    user_latest                    $2,168     $6,504
    user_daily                     $2,989     $8,968
  DynamoDB storage of the log      $8,672    $26,017
  Lambda, the projector            $1,440     $4,320
  Firehose                           $142       $426
  S3 archive, year-1 average          $73       $218
  replication transfer                $35       $105
  total                           $25,465    $76,395
  provisioned capacity at a 70% target would cut requests to $6,233 per region (-59%), but costs more than on-demand if it must cover peaks over 2.42 times the average
  not modeled: query reads, view and marker storage, backfills, archive compaction, CloudWatch

warning page_views: batches average 1.15 events over 3,469 shards, so view writes rarely combine: most events cost a row write in every view
  hint: shorten LOG RETENTION, which means fewer shards, or raise runtime.batching_window_seconds
warning page_views: ~3,469 shards per region: after an outage, the projector catches up with one invocation per shard, over Lambda's default quota of 1,000 concurrent executions, which all of the account's functions in a region share
  hint: shorten LOG RETENTION, or raise the account's Lambda concurrency quota
warning page_daily: rows reach 2.92 KB with ~119 high-water marks, one for each shard that wrote the row within the prune window (the stream has 3,469 shards); every write costs 3 write units
  hint: SPLIT 4 keeps each item at 1 write unit, saving ~$3,613 a month per region; queries then read 4 items per row
-- explain page_daily --
view page_daily, from stream page_views
  assumptions  --rate 1000  --event-kb 1  --rows page_daily=1000
               (traffic isn't in the SQL or dynaflux.yaml; set it with these flags)
  prices       us-east-1 list prices, ±30%: for comparing designs, not a quote; prices in ap-northeast-1 are higher

layout, in each of 3 regions (us-east-1, us-west-2, ap-northeast-1)
  table        analytics-view-page_daily-v1: regional, on-demand
  mode         ASYNC: the projector applies events in batches
  rows         shared: GROUP BY (page, day) leaves out user_id, the PRIMARY KEY of page_views, so any shard can write any row; ~1,000 active
  backfill     fenced handoff: count and sum are additive

row, estimated (String values are taken as 32 bytes)
  pk        page                                                     34 B
  sk        day                                                       6 B
  page      key column                                               36 B
  day       key column                                                9 B
  views     count()                                                  13 B
  total_ms  sum(duration_ms)                                         20 B
  _v        row version                                              10 B
  _hwm      ~119 high-water marks of ~24 B                        2.79 KB
  total     3 write units, 1 read unit; the item limit is 400 KB  2.92 KB

per event, per region
  rows         3.30 write units and 1.10 read units: a batch of 1.15 events updates 1.15 rows, and 10% of writes conflict and retry
  per row      ~3.3 write and 1.1 read units per second on average; a partition takes 1,000 and 3,000
  monthly      DynamoDB requests $5,781 per region, $17,343 for 3 regions

warning page_daily: rows reach 2.92 KB with ~119 high-water marks, one for each shard that wrote the row within the prune window (the stream has 3,469 shards); every write costs 3 write units
  hint: SPLIT 4 keeps each item at 1 write unit, saving ~$3,613 a month per region; queries then read 4 items per row
